A block device can discard a range in 2 modes: the plain mode and
the wide mode. In the plain mode a request that crosses a chunk
boundary is discarded as one piece or not at all. In the wide mode
such a request is discarded one block at a time, so one request
carries several chunks.

This series adds a new block layer feature BLK_FEAT_DISCARD_WIDE
that a driver sets when its device has the wide mode. With it set, the
block layer may merge discard requests that follow one another on
the device, up to the largest request the hardware takes. The merged
request is not one chunk: it carries the chunks of the requests it
was made of, and the device takes them apart again at the chunk
boundaries.

The emulation series [1] has what testing this needs.

I think the merging is worth having for whoever sends chunks one
after another, hence this RFC. What I would also like to discuss is
how userspace asks for the wide mode itself, so that it can send one
large request [2] and skip the merging. The existing flag means the
plain mode, and giving it a second meaning may confuse. Thoughts?

The logs below show it with a null_blk device, fio and blktrace:

cat /sys/block/nullb0/queue/discard_wide
mode    : 0x3
  [1:1] : 0x1   The wide mode applies to write operations
  [0:0] : 0x1   Wide Discard Supported
{..}

Writes that follow one another can be merged, as in this fio workload:

fio --name=frob-merge --filename=/dev/nullb0 \
  --rw=write --bs=16k --size=64k \
  --ioengine=io_uring \
  --iodepth=4 --iodepth_batch_submit=4 \
  --direct=1

The output below from btrace shows the four writes merged into one
request:

btrace /dev/nullb0
252,0    1        1     0.000000000  1000  Q  WS 0 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1        2     0.000001000  1000  G  WS 0 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1        3     0.000002000  1000  P   N [fio]
252,0    1        4     0.000003000  1000  Q  WS 32 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1        5     0.000004000  1000  M  WS 32 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1        6     0.000005000  1000  Q  WS 64 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1        7     0.000006000  1000  M  WS 64 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1        8     0.000007000  1000  Q  WS 96 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1        9     0.000008000  1000  M  WS 96 + 32 [fio]
252,0    1       10     0.000009000  1000  U   N [fio] 1
252,0    1       11     0.000010000  1000  D  WS 0 + 128 [fio]
252,0    1       12     0.000200000     0  C  WS 0 + 128 [0]

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20251201-emulation-v1-0-ba9876543210@example.org/ [1]
Link: https://example.org/talks/discard/ [2]

Signed-off-by: A Developer <dev@example.com>
---
A Developer (2):
      block: add BLK_FEAT_DISCARD_WIDE
      null_blk: enable the wide discard mode

 block/blk-merge.c                 |  9 ++++++++-
 block/blk-settings.c              |  5 +++++
 block/blk.h                       |  6 +++++-
 drivers/block/null_blk/main.c     | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/block/null_blk/null_blk.h |  1 +
 include/linux/blkdev.h            |  3 +++
 6 files changed, 45 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
---
base-commit: 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567
change-id: 20260101-topic-0123456789ab

